In Flask, when you load configuration from a class object via `Config.from_object()` (in `flask/config.py`), the method iterates over the class using `dir(obj)` and copies every attribute whose name is uppercase:

```python
def from_object(self, obj):
    ...
    for key in dir(obj):
        if key.isupper():
            self[key] = getattr(obj, key)
```

Because `dir()` returns the names found across the whole inheritance chain (not just the class's own `__dict__`), the parent classes' uppercase attributes are also picked up. So if a child config class inherits from a parent config class, settings defined only on the parent are still loaded into the app's config, and `getattr` resolves the value following normal Python attribute-lookup order (child overrides parent).

In other words, the inheritance chain affects configuration resolution at the point of `from_object` in `flask/config.py` — the effective configuration is the merged set of uppercase attributes visible on the class through MRO, with the most-derived class winning on conflicts.
